package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setLostCavernsOfIxalan = StorySection(
    "lci", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan",
    "Beneath the golden city of Orazca a sealed door opened onto a hollow world with a sun at its heart, and everyone who went down to claim it brought something terrible back up.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-the-hollow-world", "The Hollow World",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-a-year-after", "A Year After",
                    listOf(
                        "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan came out on November 17, 2023, under the set code LCI, with its prerelease a week earlier. It was the ninety-eighth Magic expansion and the second set of the Omenpath era, following Wilds of Eldraine. Its story takes place about a year after the [Phyrexian invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse), on a plane whose last full sets had come out in 2017 and 2018, the world of pirates, vampires, merfolk and dinosaurs. The designers have said plainly that it was never meant as an ordinary return. They wanted a set about going underground, and Ixalan was the world that fit it best.",
                        "The Ixalan of this set is a tired one. The Phyrexians had come down on every continent, and the golden city of Orazca still showed the wounds in fallen buildings, gouged walls and patched streets. The old Sun Emperor had died at the hands of a Phyrexian assassin, and a boy, Apatzec Intli IV, sat on the throne while his uncle and his aunt fought quietly over who would steer him. Across the sea, the vampire church of Torrezon was splitting into rival sects. The Brazen Coalition had turned from raiding to mining. The River Heralds had lost the Deeproot Tree.",
                        "Something else had changed too. Huatli, the empire's warrior-poet, and Saheeli Rai, the artificer who loved her, had both [lost their sparks](sparks-lost) and could no longer leave the plane. In their place came [the Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), shimmering rings that opened on Ixalan as on every other world, and through one of them a regular road had formed to Arcavios. It was along that road that Saheeli sent a letter asking for a scholar, and a loxodon archaeologist named Quintorius Kand answered.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-under-orazca", "Under Orazca",
                    listOf(
                        "After the war, people on the surface began digging, and they found that Ixalan went much further down than anyone had guessed. Imperial soldiers on patrol came across ancient chambers deep beneath Orazca, and in one of them stood a door of gold, silver and copper, its alcoves set with copper tablets carved with glyphs and inlaid with jade, cinnabar and gems. The glyphs formed a poem. Huatli translated it slowly and came to believe it promised a way to the birthplace of humanity and the home of the gods. The card {Orazca Puzzle-Door} remembers the moment it finally opened.",
                        "The pirates had gone underground for a plainer reason. The Brazen Coalition ran a mine called Downtown, a name that began as a joke and stuck. It sat in an ancient dry cenote, a vast round hole with tunnels running off it at every level like the spokes of hundreds of wheels. Cranes and catwalks reached out over the void, elevators rode up and down on pulleys, and refineries and silos crowded its rim. Downtown's ore paid for Sunray Bay, and Sunray Bay held up the whole fragile economy of the Coalition.",
                        "Below all of it lay the caverns themselves, a world of their own. There were cities built into stalactites the size of inverted mountains, deserts of sand that swallowed a man without a sound, rivers that ran through solid rock, lakes of fresh water as wide as seas, and waterfalls made of fire. People lived down there who had never seen the sky. Older peoples had died down there, and their bones still lay where they had fallen.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Between Quintorius's archaeological expertise and Huatli's knowledge of ancient glyphs, the \"impossible\" lock finally yielded, and the door to the underground world rumbled open.",
                        "Orazca Puzzle-Door",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-core", "The Core",
                    listOf(
                        "At the bottom of everything was the Core. Ixalan is hollow. Past the last door the tunnels slope down and down until the ground seems to tip over, and the traveller climbs out of a stone well into open air with clouds overhead. Quint described the feeling of crossing as dizzying, like planeswalking. Inside, the land does not end at a horizon. It curves upward in every direction and keeps climbing into the haze, so that the farthest fields hang overhead like a painted map. Amalia, the first vampire to see it, felt she was standing inside an enormous inverted globe.",
                        "The Core is not large as worlds go, a sphere perhaps twenty-two kilometres across, but it holds a whole country. There are grasslands where dinosaurs and long-necked woolly beasts graze, jungles, mountains, rivers, freshwater oceans and fetid swamps. Its people are the Oltec, and their capital Oteclan stands on the shores of Lake Wachibal, a city of canals, temples, markets and great causeways linked by cable cars. Tall towers stand at regular distances across the land to carry word between their cities.",
                        "It is the oldest place on Ixalan. The Oltec say the gods were born there and that humanity first woke there, and they mean it literally. Merfolk and the catlike Malamet are thought to have come from there too. For centuries no one from the surface had seen it, because the Oltec had sealed every way in. The card {Matzalantli, the Great Door} shows the last of those seals, the one the expedition opened.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Centuries ago, under siege from the Mycotyrant, the Oltec sealed the Core off from the world... until Huatli's party arrived.",
                        "Matzalantli, the Great Door",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-chimil-and-cosmium", "Chimil and Cosmium",
                    listOf(
                        "In the middle of the Core hangs its sun. The Oltec call her Chimil, the Riven Star, and to them she is God-Mother, the maker of the world and mother of the gods. She is close enough that Amalia imagined she could reach her with wings. She does not rise or set. Instead, broken plates of dark metal turn around her like the shards of a shell, shading half the land at any time, and behind her trails a long tail of glittering pink debris. This is the cosmium reef. {Chimil, the Inner Sun} is her card.",
                        "The metal is what is left of a cage. Long ago invaders sealed Chimil inside a shell of their ships, and when she broke free the pieces were left circling her. What poured out of her when she broke them was cosmium, a pinkish purple mineral that the expedition kept finding everywhere on the way down, in the bones of the dead, in murals, and in the knotted cords of an old poncho. Oltec scholars think it may be her blood, or her soul.",
                        "Cosmium is the great resource of the set. Refined, it acts as a battery and a conduit for magic. A cosmium blade never dulls, cosmium armour shrugs off cannon fire, and a drill painted with it grinds through any rock. The Oltec tattoo themselves with it and live longer and stronger for it. On the surface the rich of both continents began to prize it, and the vampires of Torrezon found that eating it eased their thirst for blood. Huatli herself suspected that Chimil and the Sun Empire's Threefold Sun were one and the same, and worried that the thought was heresy.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episodes 1 and 4, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Core of Paradise"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Cosmium"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-the-ages", "The Ages of the Core",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-fourth-people", "The Fourth People",
                    listOf(
                        "The Oltec count their history in ages, and they believe the world has ended and begun again more than once. Almost nothing is known of the Fourth Age, and less of the ones before it. What the Oltec remember is how it ended, and who walked out of the ending. The last survivor of the Fourth People was a mortal so afraid of death that he would do anything to escape it. The Oltec understand that he slaughtered everyone else and drank their blood to keep himself alive through the ending of all things, and that he found his way into the divine nothingness where the next world was being shaped.",
                        "There he watched the gods of the new world being born. They were the Deep Gods, the children of Chimil. He saw what he would have to do. As the last of them emerged, he killed it and drank its divinity, and became a horror, a bat god with dominion over the border between life and death. He took the name Aclazotz and went on wearing the finery of the god he had slain. The god he had murdered was reborn as a ghostly spirit known only as the Ancient One.",
                        "Then the Fifth Age began. The first humans woke inside the Core beneath Chimil and knew her as their maker. They called themselves the Komon Winaq, the Fifth People. The Core gave them everything, so they had time for study. They mapped their world, invented writing and numbers, and one of them, Tan Jolom the worldwalker, walked all the way around it and proved it was a sphere built around a star. In that age death was no terror. The veil between life and the afterlife was thin as gossamer, and the spirits of the dead, called Echoes, walked among the living.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Komon Winaq, ancestors of the Oltec, linger on as the eldest of the Echoes.",
                        "Council of Echoes",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-whispering-war", "The Whispering War",
                    listOf(
                        "For Tan Jolom, the afterlife was simply another place to explore. He became an Echo and crossed over, following a whispering voice that promised to lead him to the land of the Fourth People, hoping to find the Deep Gods and tell them their people had mapped the Core and were waiting. The voice belonged to Aclazotz. Through the door the worldwalker had left open, dark things slipped into the world, and the worst of them was the whisperer himself.",
                        "Aclazotz knew how much power there was in fear, so he hardened the veil between life and death into stone. The dead stopped coming back as Echoes. The living understood for the first time that they would die, and they grieved and were afraid, and he fed on it. In secret he found the most frightened and bereaved and whispered his promise to them. Give yourselves to me, and you will live forever. Cults rose across the Core. When elders tried to stop them they met violence, and when soldiers came the cults fought back, and the cities of the Komon burned.",
                        "The gods came at last. Ojer Axonil, drawn by the noise of battle, was the first to arrive, and he called his elder sibling Ojer Taq to his side. He raised a thousand champions and carried the war into Aclazotz's deepest lairs. When the bat god tried to kill Taq, Axonil stopped him, tore out one of his eyes and dragged him back beyond the veil, where he was bound in chains. The last of his faithful slipped away through tunnels known only to them, upward into a darker world. The veil thinned again, and the honoured dead returned.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-night-war", "The Night War",
                    listOf(
                        "What followed was the Age of the Sun, a golden age that lasted for millennia. The Komon Winaq built Oteclan on the shores of Lake Wachibal while Ojer Taq looked on. It ended when shards of dark metal appeared in the sky. One of them opened, and from it stepped a giant who spoke the Komon tongue and begged for his people to be allowed to shelter in paradise. The Komon named the strangers the Kisik. Cards of the set call them the Fomori, and the Oltec call them simply the Colonizers.",
                        "The giants did not stay guests. They ruled the Core from their ships, which hung in the sky around Chimil, and more ships came until together they closed a complete shell around her. Total darkness fell across the Core, and it cut the Komon off from the Deep Gods. That darkness lasted three hundred and twenty years. Resistance began with the Echoes and spread to the living, and its greatest hero was Olanem Teq, the Nine Hundredth Moon, who led raids out of the city of Oq'tinimit until the fighters reached the shell itself.",
                        "Chimil had been fighting too, lashing the inside of her cage. When the warriors struck the weakened shards from outside, she broke one, then another, and then the whole shell came apart in a rain of steaming fragments. Cosmium burst out of her, blasting the colonizers away and filling the Komon who stood there that day with power, so that they became the first angels of Ixalan. The Deep Gods followed, able at last to hear their mother and their people. The wreckage of the ships stayed where it fell, and some of it still circles the sun.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Ever since the Fomori's attempt long ago to extinguish Chimil's light, the Oltec have taken extensive measures to never get lost in the Core.",
                        "Cartographer's Companion",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-sealing", "The Exodus and the Seal",
                    listOf(
                        "After the Night War the Komon began to explore the caverns outside the Core, and then the world beyond them. Over centuries hundreds of thousands of them climbed all the way to the surface and stayed. Their descendants became the Sun Empire, which forgot where it had come from and taught that its people were the first of all peoples, made by the Threefold Sun to rule whatever its light touched. Those who stayed behind came to call themselves the Oltec. The Malamet say that they and the merfolk fought beside the Komon in the Night War and followed them up to the surface too.",
                        "Many centuries later a sickness struck Topizielo, one of the great Komon cities in the caverns. Oltec scholars found it was a fungus, spread by spores and far too advanced to be natural, perhaps some living relic the colonizers had left behind. It was the beginning of the mycoids. Nothing stopped it. Whole cultures in the caverns fell to it, and then to the larger and cleverer forms it grew into.",
                        "The cavern peoples fled upward and out, and those who stayed in the Core sealed every entrance behind them. A garrison of the Thousand Moons guarded the inside of the great door at Matzalantli from then on. When Abuelo, an Echo of that time, was raised again by Quint, the first thing he remembered was that Oteclan must be warned of the mycoid infestation and that the door must be closed. The Oltec call the long time since the Quiet Age, and through all of it their gardeners kept training to fight an enemy they hoped never to see again.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episodes 2 and 6, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Core of Paradise"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Coin Empire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aclazotz"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-the-deep-gods", "The Deep Gods",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-chimil-and-taq", "Chimil and Taq",
                    listOf(
                        "The Oltec have no temples to Chimil. They worship her by looking up at her. Their cities are laid out in the shape of a nine-pointed star in her honour, and in later years they added the curve of the cosmium reef that trails behind her. Most communes keep a small shrine or lantern for her in their main hall, and her glyph turns up on Oltec clothes, buildings and armour. When her light is eclipsed, so is the power of her children, all except one.",
                        "Her children are the Deep Gods, and they are alive. They can change their shapes and they walk the Core. The eldest is Ojer Taq, chief god of the Oltec and god of civilization. Taq's face is a mask inside a mask inside a mask. The outer face stands for the surface, the face within it is a jaguar for the caverns, and the innermost is a Komon woman for the Core. Taq usually appears as a tall figure in white robes who braids a long khipu that records history, the present and what may yet be, all at once.",
                        "Taq is the god of ancestors, families and posterity, the patron of stewards, cities, roads and archives, and of the Echoes. The Temple of Civilization in Oteclan is the largest temple in the Core and Taq's cult the most populous. Though Taq was the first child of Chimil born, Taq was the second to emerge, pulled through by Axonil in the Whispering War. As a card, {Ojer Taq, Deepest Foundation} multiplies the creatures it brings, which fits a god of whole peoples.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-pakpatiq", "Pakpatiq",
                    listOf(
                        "Ojer Pakpatiq is Taq's lover and the second god of the Oltec, the god of time, and in particular of time that turns in circles. He rarely bothers with a human shape. He prefers to appear as a winged serpent that loops and coils without any end. For all that his form is frightening and his domain confusing, the Oltec love him for his candour. Pakpatiq says what he means, and those who serve him try to do the same.",
                        "He is the patron of scholars, builders, scientists and spellcasters, of fisherfolk and sailors and of water in every form, and like Taq he watches over the Echoes. His priests are the didacts, teachers and explorers who run the Core's schools. Most Oltec schools have an altar or mural to him, and many of his temples double as places of advanced study. His Temple of Cyclical Time in Oteclan is the second largest in the Core and stands facing Taq's.",
                        "The didacts were the people Steward Akal Pakal offered Quint when he asked whether he could write about what he had seen. Stories are the memory of the plane, she told him, and to forget is to succumb to darkness. It was the didacts who told Quint of the colonizers, and who warned him to stay away from Colony's End. On the card {Ojer Pakpatiq, Deepest Epoch}, instants cast from the hand come back to be cast again, a small loop of time of the kind his priests study.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-axonil-and-kaslem", "Axonil and Kaslem",
                    listOf(
                        "Ojer Axonil is the younger, fiery brother of Taq, often in conflict with his sibling but never so far as to be cast out. His usual shape is a great warrior's body joined with the forms of mighty dinosaurs. In place of a head he has a naked skull ringed with gold. His right arm is the head and neck of a dinosaur tyrant, and he fights with a flaming macuahuitl. He is the god of volcanoes, fire, storms, lightning, obsidian and summer, of warriors, athletes and lamplighters. He has hurt the Oltec as often as he has saved them, yet he has always been the mightiest in their defence.",
                        "His priests are the Thousand Moons, who descend from the thousand champions he raised in the Whispering War. Axonil is the god who tore out Aclazotz's eye. On the card {Ojer Axonil, Deepest Might}, small bursts of red damage swell to the size of the god himself. When Aclazotz broke loose, it was Axonil, with his fire and his storms, that the gardeners promised would come to help.",
                        "Ojer Kaslem is the quietest of the gods. She is the god of life and growth, chimeric in body and age, cheerful and chaotic, beautiful and terrifying all at once. Her cult is the smallest and least organised, its temples scattered through deep glades and jungle valleys, and the Oltec say she is taken for granted until she is gone. She is the patron of farmers, ranchers and gardeners, and she is bound up with dinosaurs and butterflies, the monarch above all. Her gardeners came to fight the mycoids, and {Ojer Kaslem, Deepest Growth} turns her strikes into new life on the battlefield.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-betrayer", "The Great Betrayer",
                    listOf(
                        "The fifth Deep God is no child of Chimil at all. Aclazotz is the Great Betrayer, god of night and of the veil between life and death, and the eternal enemy of the sun. Unlike his false siblings he cannot change his shape. He is a giant decaying bat, as tall as three or four people standing end to end, his body the brown of old dried blood, his wings gilded and tattered, with a collar of skulls and a black and gold crown. He has one red eye. Axonil took the other.",
                        "In Oltec myth he is a trickster, a glutton, a murderer and a traitor, and very few in the Core worship him. On the surface it is another matter. Those of his faithful who escaped the Whispering War carried his promise upward, and in hidden places across the surface temples were raised to him by believers hoping to draw him out. The vampires of Torrezon hold him to be their ancient god and sire, the maker of the first vampires, and the church teaches that its first saint walked out of a door like the ones in his temples.",
                        "He had slept in his chains for ages. When he woke, he said the invaders on the surface had disturbed his slumber, and in the year after the Phyrexian war his faithful in Torrezon began to feel his pull. What he wants is simple. He means to put out Chimil for good and end the Fifth Age, and to begin a sixth and final age in which death and rebirth stop turning and he rules over all of it. The card {Aclazotz, Deepest Betrayal} is a flying, lifelinking terror that makes every opponent discard when it attacks and breeds bats from the lands they throw away.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Aclazotz laughed as the dark shards closed around Chimil, plunging the Core into unnatural darkness. At long last, his bloody reign would begin.",
                        "Malicious Eclipse",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 5, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Chimil"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Taq, Pakpatiq, Axonil and Kaslem"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aclazotz"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-the-descent", "The Descent",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-puzzle-door", "The Puzzle Door",
                    listOf(
                        "Quint stepped off his road between worlds into a wet jungle heat that reminded him of a bog back home and was nearly eaten by three feathered raptors before a young warrior called them off. Her name was Wayta. She wore a metal disk over one eye, and the largest of the dinosaurs was Pantlaza, Huatli's own companion, who trotted to her side like a pet. She led him down to Orazca, and beneath it to a painted chamber where Huatli sat surrounded by copper tablets and Saheeli greeted him with open arms.",
                        "The tablets bore coins carved among their glyphs, the very motif Quint had been tracking across plane after plane, the mark of a lost civilization he called the Coin Empire. The poem on them spoke of a full count of turnings in darkness, which Huatli worked out meant three hundred and twenty years, and of a god imprisoned. Meanwhile the emperor grew impatient. Inti, Huatli's cousin and the empire's seneschal, came down to say the throne wanted the door simply broken open. Huatli asked for a little more time.",
                        "The answer was in the door itself. Behind each tablet's alcove was a single glyph, warrior, leaf, farmer, shadow, and none of them matched any tablet directly. Huatli saw that each matched a line by meaning, the warrior with the line about fighting the betrayers, the leaf with the lost branches of the tree, and when every tablet sat in its place the door glowed and cracked open. Beyond lay a room where wrapped warriors knelt with bones glowing pinkish purple, and in the corner the corpse of a horned giant in rust-coloured armour, bristling with spears.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Between Quintorius's archaeological expertise and Huatli's knowledge of ancient glyphs, the \"impossible\" lock finally yielded, and the door to the underground world rumbled open.",
                        "Orazca Puzzle-Door",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-empty-mine", "The Empty Mine",
                    listOf(
                        "Near Sunray Bay a body lay in the leaves. Mushrooms burst from its mouth and one eye socket, black veins ran under its ashen skin, and it was rotting far faster than a man dead a few hours should. Malcolm Lee, the siren who served as Downtown's emissary and held shares in the mine, recognised a miner named Lank. Deliveries from Downtown had already stopped. Captain Vance sent Malcolm to find out why, and he went gladly, because he had friends there.",
                        "Downtown was empty. Ore sat in carts left mid-track, food rotted in the kitchens, beds lay rumpled as if their sleepers had leapt up in the night, and every elevator had been hauled to the top except one. Beside it, in blocky letters painted in ichor, was one word. DOWN. Malcolm took a small crew and his goblin companion Breeches and went down. In a side tunnel thick with glowing fungus they found bones stripped clean and dead raptors still moving, puppeted by fungal strands and breathing out spores.",
                        "Malcolm saved them by singing. His siren song stilled everything that heard it while he cut the creatures to pieces. But the pirates who had been scratched and bitten were not right afterward. Black lace spread from their wounds, began to glow green, and they started speaking in unison. On the next elevator they shoved their friends into a spore cloud and turned on everyone else, and Malcolm and Breeches had to cut the lines and let the whole platform fall into the dark. Then the fungus on the wall wrote them a message. SAFE, it said. DOWN.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-vitos-pilgrimage", "Vito's Pilgrimage",
                    listOf(
                        "A third party was already in the jungle. The Queen's Bay Company had sent about thirty people inland, vampire soldiers, human servants and penitents seeking absolution, led by the hierophant Vito Quijano de Pasamonte. With him rode Bartolomé del Presidio, a high officer of the Company, Clavileño, commander of the soldiers, and a young noble named Amalia Benavides Aguirre, who had taken the post of cartographer to do something worthwhile after the war. Amalia made her maps with cartomancy, pricking her finger and mixing the blood with ash so that the blank paper filled itself in.",
                        "Vito carried two relics that never left him, a lance once wielded by Venerable Tarrian, a companion of Saint Elenda, and Tarrian's journal, which the church had suppressed. At a ruined Temple of Aclazotz he told the expedition what it was for. Beyond a door below the temple their god and sire slept. If they woke him and brought him home to Torrezon, he would heal the faithful and end the schism tearing the Church of Dusk apart.",
                        "The door at the bottom of the catacombs would not open. Vito quoted Tarrian, saying the blood of the lamb would open the door to paradise, and had a porter laid on the obsidian altar. Bartolomé protested that this corrupted the sacred rites. Vito cut the man's throat, and the blood ran along the grooves to the door and lit it with a shining darkness until it opened. Amalia had been having visions of a sea of sand, waterfalls of fire and a round golden door, and a voice whispered to her. This was not yet her door.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The gateway could be opened only by the spilling of innocent blood. Vito did not hesitate.",
                        "Fanatical Offering",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-echo-in-the-ruins", "The Echo in the Ruins",
                    listOf(
                        "Huatli's party went down with twenty warriors under Inti and Caparocti Sunborn, the imperial champion, and half a dozen pack dinosaurs. They passed barricade after barricade where old armies had died, and crossed a chasm of glowing green fog on stone blocks that shifted underfoot. Wayta worked out the riddle of the bridge from the poem and leapt across, and when a stone fell away beneath her it was Caparocti who hauled her up. Afterward he told her that war with the Dusk Legion was as sure as day following night.",
                        "They came to a dead city in a great cavern, its stone blocks furred with glowing fungus. Its people lay where they had fallen, stripped to the bone, and some had mushrooms growing from them. Quint followed a faint pink glow to a plaza and found a poncho and a knotted cord of beads, both woven with cosmium. He cast an archaeomantic spell called The Rousing, and a teal figure of an old man rose out of the cloth. He could not remember his own name until Wayta said he looked like her grandfather, and then he knew himself. He was Abuelo.",
                        "Abuelo's first words were a warning. Oteclan must be told of the mycoid infestation, it was too late, the door must be closed. Then he fled into the ruins with Quint and Wayta running after him, and around a corner by an underground river they met a titan, a giant built of layered mushrooms with chitin spikes on its arms. It shrugged off spears and swords, regrew whatever was cut away, and spat a tar that ate through armour and flesh. Smaller mycoids poured in behind it, and warriors began to die.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Quintorius touched the old poncho he'd found. A rush of magic condensed into an Echo, and wise Abuelo stepped forth.",
                        "Abuelo's Awakening",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-sand-and-river", "Sand and River",
                    listOf(
                        "The vampires had their own road. Beyond their second door, opened with a second sacrifice, lay an underground desert where a scout walked six steps onto the sand and vanished. Clavileño, whose legs turned to smoke when he flew, marked the solid ground from above while the soldiers laid doors and broken furniture as a bridge. Pale mantis-like hunters came skating over the sand and tore porters apart before the Legion killed them. Later, by a waterfall of lava, a stranger ran toward them chased by pale goblins. Amalia changed her map with her quill, and part of the bridge beneath the goblins simply ceased to exist. The stranger was Kellan, a half-fae traveller [from Eldraine](wilds-of-eldraine), and Amalia took responsibility for him.",
                        "Then the Malamet found them. Two dozen jaguar warriors under their champion Kutzil marched the Legion to Ban Koj, a city built into a cluster of stalactites as large as inverted mountains, the first strangers to see it since the time of the Oltec. Sovereign Okinec Ahau had his mythweaver Poq look into Vito, and the vision showed a snarling face with lengthening fangs. The sovereign sentenced them all to be given to the sand. Just before she went under, Amalia told Kellan to hold his breath.",
                        "The sand carried them down and dropped them into an underground lake, where River Heralds were waiting. Huatli's people had reached the same water by another road. In the middle of the fight with the titan a wave had burst from the river, and merfolk warriors had covered the humans in a breathing spell and pushed them into the current. Both parties came up beside a great stone city of merfolk on a freshwater sea, where thousands of River Herald bands had gathered beneath a corroded golden door at the top of a stepped temple.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As surface empires send their explorers down, his hungry eyes look upward.",
                        "Sovereign Okinec Ahau",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 1, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 2, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 3, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-beyond-the-door", "Beyond the Great Door",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-matzalantli", "Matzalantli",
                    listOf(
                        "The merfolk had gathered there after the death of the Deeproot Tree, led by the Great Shaper Pashona, and they believed the golden door led to the Source. Her daughter Nicanzil, the scout who had pushed Wayta into the river, brought both parties up the endless stairs to a convocation before it. The Sun Empire warriors and the vampires saw each other and reached for their weapons. Inti wanted the invaders imprisoned and Caparocti wanted them killed. Pashona told them all they were invaders and that she could throw every one of them back into the sea.",
                        "Huatli stopped it. The merfolk wanted the door open, she said, and her people could read it, so they should work together, and she reminded them all that they had fought the Phyrexians side by side. Bartolomé surprised everyone by saying Queen Miralda might be open to such talks, and Vito told him to shut his mouth. The door used an older dialect, and beside it sat a tiered box of compartments. So Quint raised Abuelo again, who recognised the place at once. It was Matzalantli, and they must get inside to warn the Oltec of the Mycotyrant.",
                        "The key, Abuelo said, was in his wife's khipu. Quint had it. Following the knots, Huatli laid green and yellow gems in the box, and when the cosmium made Abuelo flicker and fall silent before the last step, she saw the pattern herself and laid the cosmium beads in the shape of a stylised serpent. The door swung open on cold fresh air. They walked down a tunnel that tilted the world around them and came to a well of stone full of open sky. Faces looked down over its rim, and someone said to find Anim Pakal, because the seal had been opened.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The River Heralds found clues to a grand heritage below Ixalan's surface.",
                        "Jadelight Spelunker",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-we-are-the-mycotyrant", "We Are the Mycotyrant",
                    listOf(
                        "Malcolm and Breeches went down the rest of the cenote in stages, Malcolm flying from ledge to ledge and sometimes carrying the goblin, both of them masked against the spores. At the bottom a light in the fungus led them on, guiding or herding, into a cavern big enough to hold a town. It held a forest of mushrooms as tall as trees, glowing every shade of green, their gills fluttering as the two of them passed as if they were being sniffed. In a clearing marked with rings like a web the people of Downtown came out to meet them, covered in fungus, some with mushrooms where their eyes had been.",
                        "One of them was Xavier Sal, the mayor of Downtown, and he spoke for something else. We are the Mycotyrant, he said, and behind him a huge toadlike shape lit up, hanging in a web of roots, crowned with mushrooms, with clawed arms and a mouth full of thorns. It said it knew Malcolm already, through the companions it had taken. It did not kill, it said. It changed, and it spread, and where there was one of it there were all of it. It had watched the Oltec and their gods walk the Core before the Core was denied to it, and it had traded with the Malamet and the deep goblins.",
                        "Malcolm offered a deal, gems, money, food. The Mycotyrant laughed in a cloud of spores. It wanted the sun. It had been denied the light of Chimil for ages, and there was another sun up above, and it would have it. Malcolm understood then that if this thing reached the surface it could eat Sunray Bay and perhaps all of Ixalan, and that lost gold was the least of anyone's worries. When it moved to take them, he sang again and fled toward the smell of water, with the whole colony screaming behind him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Every mycoid carries within itself the makings of an entire colony.",
                        "Tendril of the Mycotyrant",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-thousand-moons", "The Thousand Moons",
                    listOf(
                        "On the other side of the well Amalia saw the place from her visions, grasslands curving up into the sky, long-necked furry beasts grazing, and the strange sun with its broken shell and its glittering pink tail. Oltec in ponchos and khipu came out of the buildings with pink-bladed weapons, small metal gnomes waddling at their feet and Echoes drifting among them. Warriors on armoured bats came down out of the sky. Their commander was Anim Pakal, the Thousandth Moon, and her garrison had guarded the inside of Matzalantli since the door was closed in her ancestors' time.",
                        "Abuelo told her he had opened it, and why. The plan to isolate the Mycotyrant and let it die had failed, and it had grown. She was furious that they had brought ruin to her people. Huatli answered that the surface peoples were already mining deeper every year and would have found their way in regardless, and Bartolomé added that the Coalition had a whole city devoted to digging. Then Anim asked Huatli if her people were Komon, the ancestors who had left the Core long ago and never returned, and Quint guessed aloud that those travellers had reached the surface and become the Sun Empire.",
                        "The welcome ended when a soldier shouted that the others were vampires. Anim raised a shield of magic and called them worshippers of the Great Betrayer, and they were taken away. The Sun Empire party was brought to Akal Pakal, steward of the Oltec and Anim's sister, an old woman in layered robes and gold. Inti offered the strength of the empire and was eager to trade, and he asked for help getting rid of the vampires. Caparocti argued that Torrezon had never been weaker. Huatli argued for rebuilding instead of dying on foreign shores, and the two sides of the empire's argument followed them all the way into the Core.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Fomori are a foul and distant memory, but their war machines taught us much about large-scale armorcraft.",
                        "Anim Pakal, Thousandth Moon, on Master's Manufactory",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-bartolomes-stand", "Bartolomé's Stand",
                    listOf(
                        "The Oltec have no prisons, only rooms for holding people for a while, and the idea of jailing someone as punishment appalled them. Bartolomé arranged to be held with Amalia and Kellan. He was not who he seemed. Queen Miralda had sent him on the expedition to learn Vito's true loyalties, and the relics Vito carried made him fear that the faction gathering around Vona de Iedo, the Antifex, was larger than the queen had hoped. In the cell Kellan asked whether vampires murdered people and drank their blood, and Amalia insisted that her church fed only on criminals. Kellan pointed out that right now they were the criminals.",
                        "From the next room Vito preached to his soldiers. Fog rolled over the garrison, thick enough to swallow a hand. Screams came through the walls and stopped, and the smell of blood reached Amalia. The soldiers had not fed enough on the way down, and their servants were gone. Vito tore open the door with blood on his mouth and said Aclazotz demanded a sacrifice, and that the blood of the outsider would do. He meant Kellan.",
                        "Bartolomé had asked Amalia to swear that if anything happened to him she would go home and tell the queen everything. Then he threw himself at Vito with his hands curled into claws, blocking the doorway so nobody else could get through. Vito broke his neck. With a shaking hand, Amalia drew her quill along the edge of the building on her map and erased the wall behind her, and she and Kellan ran out into the fog while Vito's people went looking for their god.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 4, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 5, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Mycotyrant"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-the-eclipse", "The Eclipse",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-reef", "The Reef",
                    listOf(
                        "For a short while things were peaceful. The Oltec invited the Sun Empire warriors to share in their coming-of-age ceremony, in which young Oltec ride bats up to the cosmium reef behind Chimil and try to bring back a piece of it. Quint stayed on the ground to mind Pantlaza and talk with Steward Akal, who told him the Oltec didacts were explorers and teachers much like him, and offered him one to help, in exchange for stories from his own world. Huatli came back bouncing with a pink crystal in her hand, then went quiet when Akal suggested she set it in a sword.",
                        "Wayta flew back past them all. The vampires, she reported, had summoned a fog far bigger than usual. Anim Pakal arrived with two prisoners, Amalia and Kellan, who had been running from the garrison to find help rather than to escape. They told the Oltec everything. The other vampires had murdered their servants and the queen's envoy and gone to find Aclazotz. Akal turned pale. Some Oltec still worshipped the Betrayer, she said, and if the vampires found them they might at last be strong enough to set him free.",
                        "At the same time, far below, Malcolm burst out of a tunnel above the River Heralds' sea shouting a warning that he put magic into so it would carry, with Breeches under his arm and the whole colony behind him. Infected miners, rotten dinosaurs, fungus-crusted Malamet and walking mushrooms spilled onto the beach, and flying things clotted with fungus came pouring out of holes in the roof. The merfolk called up elementals of fire and water to fight them. Nicanzil said the people beyond the golden door had to be warned, and Malcolm and Breeches ran for it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-aclazotz-unchained", "Aclazotz Unchained",
                    listOf(
                        "In the fog a figure with a red glow found Vito and said it had been sent to guide him. It led the vampires out of the garrison and down into a fetid swamp, where more of its kind joined them, people carrying dim red crystals and dragging glassy-eyed prisoners through the mud. They were the cosmium eaters, Oltec who had worshipped Aclazotz through all the ages of his imprisonment. Vito had believed his god was only biding his time. He learned now that the father of vampires had been in chains.",
                        "The temple was hidden behind a plain cave in a mountain. Its door asked for a key of pink crystal and then for the worth of the one who placed it, and it clamped down on Vito's arm and drained him until he could barely stand. He did not fall, and the door rolled aside. Inside, in an amphitheatre lit red by glowing glyphs, a thousand pale faces turned toward him, and at the centre crouched Aclazotz, wrapped in golden chains set with hundreds of pink crystals.",
                        "Aclazotz told them the invaders on the surface had disturbed his sleep and that his time had come. The prisoners were fed to him one by one. With every death his eye grew brighter, until the crystals in his chains dimmed to red and the links burst apart. He told his faithful to come and receive his blessing. Vito went first. The god sank his fangs into him, and Vito's bones cracked and reformed, and when it was over he stood with wings and claws and the face of a bat. Clavileño and the rest changed after him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Though officially deemed a heretic for his reverent writings on Aclazotz, Tarrian is viewed as a holy martyr by the most radical sects of the Church of Dusk.",
                        "Tarrian's Journal",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-sun-goes-out", "The Sun Goes Out",
                    listOf(
                        "Aclazotz flew up out of the mountain with his new children behind him, straight to the reef. There he spoke to Chimil. As he had been put in his prison, he told her, so would she be. He would consume her precious Oltec as he had their ancestors, end the Fifth Age at last, and his children would bring the Sixth Age to the plane. He spread his wings and began to close them slowly, as if pulling an enormous weight, and the broken shell around the sun began to move.",
                        "The shards turned and locked together like a shattered vessel being put back together. A shadow darker than any night in Torrezon fell across the Core. Then Aclazotz dropped to the ground with a sound like thunder, exhausted, the job half done, with shafts of light still slipping between the plates. He needed more sacrifices, he said, and Vito promised them. Across the land, in every garrison and village, the Oltec lit fires and lanterns and called light to themselves rather than bow to the dark.",
                        "Wayta rode toward the fight with the old fear of the Phyrexian war rising in her, when the enemy had blotted out the sky and her friends had died screaming around her. Huatli flew up beside her with her shard of the Threefold Sun shining on her armour and told her to breathe, that victory would be theirs. The Sun Empire riders and the Oltec flew into the reef together against the bat-winged things that had been the Dusk Legion, some of them still wearing their helmets and plate, and some of them Oltec in torn ponchos.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Aclazotz laughed as the dark shards closed around Chimil, plunging the Core into unnatural darkness. At long last, his bloody reign would begin.",
                        "Malicious Eclipse",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-death-of-inti", "The Death of Inti",
                    listOf(
                        "In the reef Vito fought Inti, the seneschal of the sun. Inti's shield knocked his lance aside again and again, and once he turned the light on his belt full on Vito and drove him back hissing. He did not see Clavileño gliding up behind him. Vito boasted that his god had given him something greater than Inti's ever had, the power of flight, and drove his lance through the skull of Inti's bat. While Inti fought to unbuckle himself from his falling mount, Clavileño took his head in one claw and broke his neck, and man and bat fell together into the dark.",
                        "Huatli saw it and dived after him. She landed beside her cousin's body and held him in the blood-spattered soil of a land far from home, and every word of comfort she had ever offered another family turned to sand in her mouth. Vito came down after her. He meant to take her to Aclazotz as a sacrifice, or perhaps let her live to write the story of his victory.",
                        "He would not stop talking. He told her he would be made a saint, that he and Vona would rule at their master's side and purge the faithful who would not accept the true rites, and that the Sun Empire would soon be only a memory. Huatli said nothing. She was busy. She reached out with her magic to the mountains and forests and fields around her, calling, and the voices that answered grew so many that her head swam. The ground began to tremble beneath her feet.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Though he wept at Inti's death, Caparocti's eyes were keen, and his spear pierced cleanly through Clavileño's tainted heart.",
                        "Bitter Triumph",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-huatlis-final-strike", "Huatli's Final Strike",
                    listOf(
                        "Vito struck down at her from the air, and Huatli caught his lance in the notch of her shield and twisted it out of his hand. Only death was inevitable, she told him, even for him. Then a flying dinosaur slammed into him out of the sky, and Pantlaza came racing out of the dark to rake him with the great claws on his feet. More dinosaurs came by land and air, and every time Vito tried to rise they drove him back down.",
                        "When he threw one off and opened a gap, Huatli charged with the lance of Tarrian, the relic he had carried all the way from Torrezon, and drove it through his armour and his heart and pinned him to the ground. He asked his god why he had been forsaken, and the red light went out of his eyes. The dinosaurs gathered around her afterward and nuzzled her with their snouts and feathers, and Pantlaza crooned to her like a parent to a hurt child.",
                        "It did not bring Inti back, and the fight was not over. Huatli sent the flying dinosaurs up into the reef and the rest toward her allies, mounted her bat, and promised her cousin she would come back for him when every vampire in the Core was dead. The card {Huatli's Final Strike} is this moment, and a set card called {Bitter Triumph} says that Caparocti avenged Inti too, putting a spear through Clavileño's heart.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Inti's name will grace our poems and histories for generations. Yours will collect dust, forgotten, in this cave.",
                        "Huatli to Vito, Huatli's Final Strike",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-gardeners-fire", "The Gardeners' Fire",
                    listOf(
                        "The mycoids came pouring through Matzalantli, hundreds of them or thousands, with Malcolm flying ahead of them. Wayta met him in the sky, and they traded the news in a breath. Her vampires had turned into bat monsters and their god had locked up the sun. His giant mushroom was turning people into puppets to take over the plane. Steward Akal sent for the gardeners, the order of Ojer Kaslem, who had spent the whole Quiet Age preparing for exactly this enemy. Quint used Abuela's khipu to raise Abuelo's wife, and she sniffed the air and said the Mycotyrant was near.",
                        "The gardeners came with staves tipped with cosmium and with Echoes of their own bound to necklaces, masks and blades, and they sent word that Ojer Kaslem and Ojer Axonil would help. Amalia, at the gardeners' direction, drew a fissure on her map, and the ground split open across the path of the fungal army. The mycoids grafted themselves together into a living bridge and crossed it anyway. So the Echoes went into them. Each spirit slid into a mycoid and made itself a sickness, and the creature split with teal light and dissolved into smoke. The gardeners stood in threes and sent out rings of pink light that set every fungus they touched on fire.",
                        "The Mycotyrant itself hung back across the fissure, held up in its web by two titans. Breeches had been carrying a weapon he had taken as payment for a debt in High and Dry, a metal tube etched with vines with a leaf for a trigger and petals at its mouth, more dangerous than it was worth. Malcolm flew him over the battle, and after nearly shooting Malcolm with it, the goblin fired. A ball of sticky fire tore through the titan and brought the Mycotyrant down burning, and across the field the mycoids shrieked as one and collapsed like puppets with their strings cut.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-temple-breaks", "The Temple Breaks",
                    listOf(
                        "Then red lightning burst from the distant mountains, a whole face of rock sheared away in a landslide, and the ground shook people off their feet. As the dust cleared, the shell around the sun began, very slowly, to drift apart, and the first light of a strange dawn came through. Where the mountain had fallen away stood the pillars of a great temple glowing blood red. Amalia clutched her head and knew it at once as the temple of Aclazotz.",
                        "Anim Pakal led the Thousand Moons up the mountain, with Amalia and Kellan fighting beside them through cosmium eaters and changed vampires, and dinosaurs patrolling the slopes with blood on their claws. They found the temple broken open, the golden chains scattered as if blown apart, and the place stinking of blood. Aclazotz was gone, and so were his children. A captured cosmium eater spat at Anim and promised that the Betrayer was free and gathering his children, and that the Fifth Age would soon end in blood. He called Amalia a traitor who had run when she was called.",
                        "Something in Amalia broke then. She had been raised to believe her god was distant but kind, and she found he was neither. Kellan, who said he understood that better than anyone there, told her she was not locked into a destiny someone else had planned, and that if she did not like her god she could look for another one. She asked him to tell her about his gods. He said they did not have those where he came from, but he could tell her about the fae, and they walked back down toward the growing dawn.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 5, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 6, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aclazotz"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-what-came-up", "What Came Up",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-feast-at-oteclan", "The Feast at Oteclan",
                    listOf(
                        "A week after the battle a second Sun Empire delegation arrived at Oteclan. Chimil burned as brightly as ever, the pyres of the dead burned beside her, the River Heralds had gone back to their sea, and the Oltec had begun cleaning up the mess everyone else had made of their home. The new diplomats were mostly warriors, all of them loyal to the emperor, and Caparocti sat at Steward Akal's left hand as the emperor's voice. Quint offered Wayta an eavesdropping spell, and she accepted.",
                        "What they heard was ugly. Huatli begged to mourn and rebuild rather than look for new battles, and said Aclazotz could be hunted by a small force without declaring war on the whole Legion of Dusk. Caparocti mocked her, asked whether she meant to bore a god to death with poetry, and said her title had been stolen for her by her cousin Inti rather than granted by the late emperor. Huatli stood and told him to keep Inti's name out of his mouth, and that he shared the vampires' thirst for blood, and walked out. Anim argued that the Oltec could no longer ignore the surface. Akal answered only that they would do what must be done.",
                        "Huatli found Wayta later by a lake and gave her Inti's sword, with a cosmium crystal now set in its pommel. Inti was an Echo, she said, and his spirit lived in that gem, and Wayta could ask him herself whether he approved. She told Wayta she had been invited to Otepec by the boy emperor's aunt, Caztaca Huicintli, and that she was not the only one in no hurry to light a new war while the embers of the last were still warm.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-roads-apart", "Roads Apart",
                    listOf(
                        "The Brazen Coalition party climbed back to the surface with Amalia and Kellan among them, which was nearly worse than the way down. Malcolm had gone looking for survivors and was coming home knowing there were none. He reached Sunray Bay hoping for a bath, a bed and a tankard to drown the taste of failure, and found it as empty as Downtown, buildings burnt out, food spoiled, and mushrooms glowing green in its cracks. There were no ships at the docks. He and Breeches set off up the coast to find any boat that floated, to warn High and Dry if it was not already too late.",
                        "Amalia had sent word of what Aclazotz might bring to her family and to Queen Miralda through the surviving Company members. She did not take it home herself. She sailed with Kellan to a lush island, and beyond its jungle they found a ring of light hanging above a grassy field, an Omenpath. He had no idea where it led and asked whether she was really ready to leave her whole world behind. She was. They took each other's hands and jumped through together.",
                        "Kellan's search for his father went on. The card {Kellan, Daring Traveler} shows him in this set, still on the road that began in a village on Eldraine, and {Amalia Benavides Aguirre} shows the cartographer whose faith broke in the Core. Malcolm and Breeches have their own cards, {Malcolm, Alluring Scoundrel} and {Breeches, Eager Pillager}, a siren who sings enemies still and a goblin who believes every problem can be solved with a big enough explosion.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-colonys-end", "Colony's End",
                    listOf(
                        "Quint stayed. The didacts told him the old stories of the colonizers, giants whose great dark vessels had come out of the sky and caged Chimil, and they warned him to keep away from a place called Colony's End, which had never been fully explored. He went anyway, alone, telling himself he knew how to be careful. Colony's End was half of a huge circular ship sticking out of a mountainside, seemingly cast from a single piece of metal, and on its wall were the coin carvings he had followed across the Multiverse.",
                        "Inside it was cold, sealed tighter than any tomb, without dust or rust or mould, and empty except for tall oblong shapes that might have been furniture made for giants. He mapped room after room. On the lower level he found a long row of great tanks with their fronts shattered and nothing left inside. At the end of the row one tank was whole. He wiped the glass with his handkerchief and pressed his face to it, and the tank lit up with a deep clang.",
                        "Smoke inside it cleared to reveal a creature so tall Quint could not see its head, only thick grey legs and clawed hands, like the giant corpse they had found in the first chamber under Orazca. It was far better preserved. He was already planning how to bring a team to move it when its fingers twitched, and stretched, and closed into a fist. Quint decided that he should go, right now. It was the first living trace anyone had found of the Coin Empire.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Ever since the Fomori's attempt long ago to extinguish Chimil's light, the Oltec have taken extensive measures to never get lost in the Core.",
                        "Cartographer's Companion",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-what-escaped", "What Escaped",
                    listOf(
                        "The Mycotyrant had not died. The thing Breeches burned was only one of its bodies, and its mind lived on in every spore. In the last pages of the story it thinks of itself as one mind spread through many, puzzled that the masked pirates still refuse to join it. It has learned from the battle that stealth can succeed where force failed. On the surface, vampires clearing a jungle temple break open a fungal sac and breathe in what comes out. A new body that keeps its old shape, except for dark lenses over its eyes, stands on the deck of a ship sailing for High and Dry, the Coalition's capital.",
                        "Aclazotz had escaped too. He lay in the reeking hold of a ship bound for Torrezon, hungry to stretch his wings and hunt, with terrified prisoners penned around him for his followers to feed on. Vito was dead and would be forgotten. The vampire the god wanted now was Vona de Iedo, the Antifex, who had rejected the church's teachings on her own. He meant to set her at his right wing, take Torrezon, and then come back for Chimil and finish her.",
                        "So the expedition that went looking for the home of the gods came back up having freed two horrors onto the surface, one that wanted every living thing joined to it and one that wanted every vampire remade in his own image. The cards {The Mycotyrant} and {Vito, Fanatic of Aclazotz} show them at the height of their power in the Core. Neither of their stories ended here.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Due to archaic Brazen Coalition guidelines regarding hats, a few mycoids are now technically pirate captains.",
                        "Squirming Emergence",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episode 6, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Coin Empire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Mycotyrant"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-pawns", "Pawns",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-a-spark-gone", "A Spark Gone",
                    listOf(
                        "A side story called Pawns tells what Saheeli was doing while Huatli was underground. It begins on a lonely beach on Ixalan's northern coast, where Saheeli tries to planeswalk and feels nothing. Huatli has lost her spark too, and she describes what is left the way others did across the Multiverse, a hole, like a missing limb that has been sunburnt. Saheeli has a theory. The basic stuff of reality, mana and aether, can be moved but never destroyed, so their sparks must have gone somewhere. Huatli says that if they were taken, someone can get them back, and offers to explore their world the slow way, together.",
                        "Then the court of the Sun Empire calls. The boy emperor Apatzec Intli IV clutches a stuffed quetzacama on his throne while two adults fight over him, his uncle Atlacan Huicintli, who runs the day to day of the capital, and his aunt Caztaca Huicintli, the Apex Priest of the Threefold Sun. Saheeli has been building filigree dinosaurs for the emperor, which Atlacan calls mechanoquetzacama, and Atlacan wants them faster. It is Atlacan who orders Huatli down into the newly found chambers under Orazca, alongside Caparocti and his lancers.",
                        "In a quiet corner of the market Huatli tells Saheeli the truth. Atlacan wants to restart his father's war on Torrezon and is already building a second Dawn Fleet at Queen's Bay. Huatli and others mean to stop him by putting Caztaca in power at the boy's side. It is a coup. Saheeli admits that she told the council she had sent to Ghirapur for help, when in truth the only Omenpath open led to Arcavios, and that a planeswalker named Quintorius Kand is coming. Huatli says she may find a use for him.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-lighthouse-pact", "The Lighthouse Pact",
                    listOf(
                        "Days after Huatli went below, her allies smuggled Saheeli out of Orazca, through Queen's Bay among the raw hulls of the new fleet, and across the ocean in a mixed crew of Sun Empire sailors and Brazen Coalition pirates. Saheeli spent most of the week sick in her bunk. On board was Caztaca herself, severe and gliding, guarded by her temple warriors, and she asked Saheeli sharp questions. Who controlled the filigree dinosaurs? Whoever held their command codes, Saheeli said, and the master keys were in her workshop in Pachatupa.",
                        "In a storm-battered lighthouse cabin on a remote island, Caztaca met Elenda, the first vampire and saint of the Church of Dusk, who was trying to hold her church together against the rising faction of Vona. They were interrupted by Admiral Beckett Brass of the Brazen Coalition, who drove her cutlass into the plank floor and offered her ships to both their causes in return for one thing, a pirate nation of her own in the ocean between their lands. Caztaca gave her a feather and promised that recognition once she ruled. Saheeli wrote the agreement down, and all three signed.",
                        "Then Saheeli sealed the document inside a seamless case of copper, silver and gold spun from the soldiers' coins, a case only she could open without destroying the paper inside, and Elenda took it away as a receipt. The new order of the world had been decided in about thirty minutes. Saheeli found she hated how messy it was, and decided that the answer was not to stand apart from it but to pick up a sword. The card {Saheeli, the Sun's Brilliance} shows her in this set.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "To the mother lode, and death take the wary!",
                        "Captain Storm, Cosmium Raider",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Pawns, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-peoples", "Peoples of the Deep",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-oltec", "The Oltec",
                    listOf(
                        "The Oltec are the people of the Core and the living ancestors of the Sun Empire. They see themselves as the first people of Ixalan, made by Chimil and shepherded by her children. Unlike the empire on the surface they are no empire at all. They are a constellation of communes, equal city-states joined by trade, shared history and purpose, and they think of themselves as the guardians of the Core and nothing more. They are about as numerous as the surface empires. They wear ponchos and khipu, play a ball game, and are served by little cosmium-powered metal servants called gnomes, built on designs taken from the colonizers.",
                        "They are the only people who have truly mastered cosmium, and it turned them from a small farming society into perhaps the most advanced culture on or under Ixalan. They rarely wear armour because cosmium protects them. A young Oltec's first tattoo, inked in cosmium, comes at the end of a long coming-of-age trial that finishes with a bat ride to the reef around Chimil to gather the cosmium needed. It gives them longer lives, better health and stronger magic.",
                        "Their dead do not entirely leave. Honoured Oltec return as Echoes, spirits bound to objects they owned, and walk among the living, and the Oltec consult them. Some Echoes are clear and nearly solid, some are wisps, and some are monsters changed by their binding. Abuelo was bound to his poncho and his wife to her khipu, and the Echo of Inti was set in the gem of his sword.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The folk of the Sun Empire respect their ancestors. The Oltec go further, communing with spirits of esteemed elders after death.",
                        "Didact Echo",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-stewards-and-orders", "Stewards and Orders",
                    listOf(
                        "Oltec leaders are stewards, elected by the communes they represent. Stewards sit on regional councils, and those councils each send a representative to the Level Council, the highest body of the Oltec. Only someone who has made their first pilgrimage to Chimil may stand. Most stewards are elders, as Akal Pakal is, and they wear rich robes and crowns etched with the glyphs of their communes. The card {Akal Pakal, First Among Equals} carries the Oltec idea of leadership in its very name.",
                        "Each of the gods has an order. The gardeners serve Ojer Kaslem. They tend the wild places, gather medicinal plants, keep up the old ruins and the stones where Echoes dwell, watch for fires and minister to far-flung farmers, and they travel with llamas and staves tipped with cosmium. They also train to fight mycoids. The didacts serve Ojer Pakpatiq, and they are the teachers and explorers of the Core, never far from a scroll.",
                        "The Thousand Moons serve Ojer Axonil. They are the Oltec's only professional soldiers, exactly one thousand of them, ranked so that every tenth moon is an officer, every hundredth a captain, and the last the supreme commander, the Thousandth Moon. In a peaceful society they are honoured outliers. Then there are the cosmium eaters, who are not an order at all but a scattering of secret cults to Aclazotz, people driven by greed or hate or the fear of death, who carry dim red crystals as their sign. {Anim Pakal, Thousandth Moon} leads the first. The second freed a god.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Dusk Legion invaders sought lone Oltec travelers to prey upon. The Core's guidestones did not approve.",
                        "Oteclan Landmark",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-malamet", "The Malamet",
                    listOf(
                        "The Malamet are a people of jaguarfolk who live in the caverns, taller and broader than humans, with spotted fur, cat faces and fangs that make a vampire's teeth look tame. They fight with bows, obsidian blades and polearms carved with glyphs, and their magic draws glowing glyphs in the air that mimic the spots on their fur and bind their enemies like chains. Their greatest city is Ban Koj, built into stalactites the size of upside-down mountains, its streets slung on rope bridges and nets, with carriages hanging from cables.",
                        "By their own telling the Malamet once fought beside the Komon in the Night War and followed them up to the surface, and they had empires there until the young Sun Empire took them, and they were driven back down into the dark. They had not let a stranger into Ban Koj since the time of the Oltec. Their sovereign Okinec Ahau told the vampires there were no gods there but him, and threw them all into the sand. His champion {Kutzil, Malamet Exemplar} is the warrior who captured them.",
                        "The mycoids were their enemy as much as anyone's, and the Mycotyrant itself said it had traded with them. Some of the cat warriors in its army were Malamet it had taken. A card from the set records that the Oltec and the Malamet, wary of each other at first, found common cause against the fungus and began to share their glyph magic. {Sovereign Okinec Ahau} is the ruler himself.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Invaders in the West Chasm? Give me an hour and they'll be fungus food.",
                        "Kutzil, Malamet Exemplar",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-heralds-and-goblins", "Heralds and Goblins",
                    listOf(
                        "The River Heralds came down into the caverns grieving. The Deeproot Tree, the great sacred tree at the heart of their faith, had died, and afterward the merfolk bands of the whole plane gathered in the deeps. Under the Great Shaper Pashona they found a stone city on a freshwater sea and settled there by the thousands, the largest gathering of merfolk Ixalan had ever known, to wait for the opening of the final gate. They believed it led to the Source.",
                        "They were the ones who saved Huatli's people from the titan, wrapping them in a spell that let them breathe water and sending them down an underground river, and they were the first to face the full fungal army on their own shore. They called up walking bonfires and waterspouts to fight it. Their old grudges with the Sun Empire over Orazca were still raw, but they worked with Huatli at the door all the same. {Nicanzil, Current Conductor} is Pashona's daughter, the scout who brought both parties up the stairs.",
                        "The caverns hold other peoples too. Pale, hairless goblins, called deep goblins, chased Kellan across a bridge by a fall of lava and hid in the cliffside buildings around the vampires' road, and the Mycotyrant claimed to have traded with them as well. There were mantis-like hunters in the sand sea that dismembered their prey with razor forearms, and inside the Core there were bats large enough to ride into battle.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The River Heralds found clues to a grand heritage below Ixalan's surface.",
                        "Jadelight Spelunker",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-mycoids", "The Mycoids",
                    listOf(
                        "The mycoids are a fungus that infests the living. The smallest are spores, the largest are titans, and in between there are walking mushroom folk with crude weapons, rotting dinosaurs and bats puppeted by fungal strands, and people. All of them are one mind, which calls itself the Mycotyrant. Its great toadlike body in its web of roots was only a nexus. Its real self is the whole network, which thinks of itself as one titanic being spread across a world.",
                        "It prizes knowledge above all and gets it by consuming and copying. What it takes in shapes what it becomes, and from its victims it has learned tools, weapons and even magic. After the miners of Downtown, it found ships very useful. It honestly believes joining it is good for its victims, because it makes them more efficient and joins their minds together, and it does not seem to understand that it is killing them. It wants the sun, any sun, because it had been shut away from Chimil's light for ages.",
                        "The Oltec think it began as a disease at Topizielo, perhaps a living relic of the colonizers. They spent centuries preparing for its return. Their Echoes can turn themselves into a counter infection that only harms mycoids, and their gardeners burn it with cosmium light. {The Mycotyrant} on its card is as large as the colony of Fungi around it, and it spawns new Fungus each turn for every time you descended.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Every mycoid carries within itself the makings of an entire colony.",
                        "Tendril of the Mycotyrant",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-coin-empire", "The Coin Empire",
                    listOf(
                        "Quint came to Ixalan chasing a ghost. On plane after plane he had found the same coin motif carved into ruins, the mark of a civilization that had once spread across many worlds and then vanished, and having no other name for it he called it the Coin Empire. The tablets on the door under Orazca carried those coins. So did the wall of Colony's End.",
                        "On Ixalan these people were the Kisik, or the Colonizers. Cards from the set give their real name, the Fomori. They were grey-skinned horned giants in rust-coloured armour, with hands the size of a person ending in curved talons. They came in great semicircular ships, each seemingly made from one piece of metal, with tall rooms, oversized furniture and tanks where some of them slept. They asked for refuge in the Core and then took it, and caged its sun.",
                        "The giant corpse in the first chamber beneath Orazca, killed by kneeling warriors, was one of them. The Night War memorial in Oteclan holds more. And in the last intact tank at Colony's End, one was still alive, or came alive when Quint touched the glass. What it was and why it woke, the set does not say. {Master's Manufactory} and {Cartographer's Companion} carry Oltec memories of the Fomori in their flavor text.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Fomori are a foul and distant memory, but their war machines taught us much about large-scale armorcraft.",
                        "Anim Pakal, Thousandth Moon, on Master's Manufactory",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episodes 1 to 6, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Oltec"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Coin Empire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Mycotyrant"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-the-expedition", "The Expedition",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-huatli", "Huatli",
                    listOf(
                        "Huatli is the warrior-poet of the Sun Empire, the voice that records its history and rouses its people, and a woman who can call dinosaurs with her magic. She once had a spark and travelled the Multiverse, and lost it after the invasion, and she now lives with Saheeli Rai in Orazca. In this story she is a translator and a scholar first. She wants peace above all and has had enough of war, which is an awkward thing for the empire's warrior-poet to want, and she is secretly working to stop the next one.",
                        "Under the ground she keeps saying wise things in the old style of the empire. The tree with shallow roots will not weather the storm. The one who dies having made no mistakes never lived. The stone does not feel each drop of rain, but still it is worn away. She is also the one who solves the puzzle doors, who talks the merfolk and the empire into working together, and who kills Vito with his own relic after he kills her cousin.",
                        "By the end she has quarrelled openly with Caparocti, given Inti's sword to Wayta, and accepted an invitation from Caztaca, still unable to find the words to send her people to war. The card {Huatli, Poet of Unity} shows her in this set, and its back face is a saga named for the Fifth People. {Huatli's Final Strike} is her vengeance.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-quint", "Quint",
                    listOf(
                        "Quintorius Kand is a loxodon of Strixhaven's Lorehold college, an archaeologist and archaeomancer whose spark lit during the Phyrexian invasion. Unlike his hosts he still has it. He is cheerful, curious, clumsy and talkative, licks old bones to see what they are made of, and stuffs his pockets with fruit at dinner. He once found the lost loxodon city of Zantafar with the help of his spirit mentor Asterion, who did not survive the dig.",
                        "His magic raises the past. With The Rousing he called Abuelo out of a poncho, and later Abuela out of her khipu, and in battle he fought with scrolls, one hovering in front of him as a shield and another lashing out like a whip of golden energy. He was the one who wanted to set up a proper excavation, write it all up and perhaps publish it across the Multiverse, and Wayta's challenge, that this was not his story to tell, stayed with him for the rest of the journey.",
                        "He is the only planeswalker card in the set, {Quintorius Kand}, and he comes with his own showcase treatment. His real goal was always the Coin Empire, and he left Ixalan's story having found more questions than answers, and one giant that should have been dead.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-wayta", "Wayta",
                    listOf(
                        "Wayta is a young Sun Empire warrior who lied about her age to join the army and lost an eye defending Ixalan from the Phyrexians at Tocatli. After the invasion she could not bear the jungles full of her dead friends, so she went to sea with the Brazen Coalition, scraping barnacles at High and Dry and sometimes helping the pirates steal and sell artifacts, including her own people's. She came home a year older and joined the expedition looking for a distraction.",
                        "She is quiet and dry and sharp. She leapt the glyph bridge, fought the mycoid titan alone until help came, and challenged Quint about who had the right to tell her people's stories. Once she had dreamed of being a warrior-poet herself before the war took that from her, and she writes small honest poems anyway. Not everyone has to be a legendary hero, she told Huatli, because a candle isn't as bright as the Threefold Sun but it still lights a room.",
                        "She ended the story carrying Inti's sword with his Echo inside it. Her card is {Wayta, Trainer Prodigy}, printed in the set's Commander decks, and it suits the girl who called off three raptors with a flick of the hand in the very first scene.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-amalia-and-kellan", "Amalia and Kellan",
                    listOf(
                        "Amalia Benavides Aguirre is a young vampire noble who would rather be in her family's library, and who took a cartographer's post to do something worthwhile. Her cartomancy goes beyond drawing maps. With a stroke of her quill she can change the map and the world with it, vanishing half a bridge or a whole wall, or splitting the earth into a fissure. On the way down she kept hearing a whispering voice and seeing visions of places she had never been, the voice of Aclazotz calling to his own, and she ran from it.",
                        "Kellan is the half-fae son of a planeswalker, a young man from Eldraine with two hilts that grow blades of golden light, who walked through an Omenpath looking for his father and found himself in the caverns of Ixalan, chased by goblins. Amalia saved him and vouched for him, and he kept being ambushed, captured and thrown into things. He is cheerful about it. The worst monster he can think of is a giant goose.",
                        "The two grew close. Bartolomé died protecting them, and they told the Oltec the truth. When her faith broke Kellan was the one who talked her through it, and she chose to walk through the next Omenpath with him rather than go home. Her card {Amalia Benavides Aguirre} turned out to be one of the most powerful in the set, and his is {Kellan, Daring Traveler}, an adventure card whose adventure half is named Journey On.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-malcolm-and-breeches", "Malcolm and Breeches",
                    listOf(
                        "Malcolm Lee is a siren and a former pirate who sailed with Vraska before the war and was nearly relieved afterward to settle into a steadier life as Downtown's emissary for the Brazen Coalition. He can dowse for ore, fly on his own magic where the air does not move, and sing so that everyone who hears him stands still. He keeps catching himself hoping, and the caverns keep punishing him for it.",
                        "Breeches is a blue-furred goblin in a three-cornered hat who carries a blade in each hand and one in his tail, and who mostly speaks in loud, short shouts about gold, gems and explosions. Every time trouble came he asked whether it was time for a big boom. Usually Malcolm said no. In the end it was, and his flowered tube of fire brought down the Mycotyrant's nexus.",
                        "Of the eight pirates who went down with them only those two came back, and they came home to an empty town. The cards are {Malcolm, Alluring Scoundrel} and {Breeches, Eager Pillager}, and the Coalition's old admiral leads one of the four Commander decks.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-vito-and-the-legion", "Vito and the Legion",
                    listOf(
                        "Vito Quijano de Pasamonte was a hierophant of the Church of Dusk who believed the church had betrayed its true god. He read Tarrian's suppressed journal over and over, carried Tarrian's lance like a standard, and was sure he alone had been chosen. He despised Saint Elenda's teachings of humility and restraint and wanted his people to embrace their strength. He cut throats to open doors, murdered Bartolomé, received Aclazotz's blessing first, and died asking why he had been forsaken. {Vito, Fanatic of Aclazotz} is his card.",
                        "Bartolomé del Presidio was the opposite kind of vampire, an elegant officer of the Queen's Bay Company with a bladed whip, sent by Queen Miralda to watch Vito. He saw his own daughter in Amalia and resolved to protect her whatever it cost, and it cost him his life. He is {Bartolomé del Presidio} in the set. Clavileño, the cold, fang-baring commander of the soldiers, became a bat-winged demon and killed Inti, and {Clavileño, First of the Blessed} leads one of the Commander decks.",
                        "Behind them all stood a church at war with itself. Queen Miralda and the old scripture held one side. Vona de Iedo, the Antifex, a vampire who had already rejected the church's teachings, led the other, and it was her that Aclazotz chose in the end. Saint Elenda, the very first vampire, was trying to hold everything together.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Though officially deemed a heretic for his reverent writings on Aclazotz, Tarrian is viewed as a holy martyr by the most radical sects of the Church of Dusk.",
                        "Tarrian's Journal",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Episodes 1 to 6, by Valerie Valdes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, Pawns, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Quintorius Kand"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-discover", "Discover",
                    listOf(
                        "Discover is the set's new way to get free spells, a reworked version of cascade. It is a keyword action that always comes with a number. To discover a number, you exile cards from the top of your library until you exile a nonland card with mana value that number or less. You may cast that card without paying its mana cost, or simply put it into your hand. Everything else exiled goes to the bottom of your library in a random order.",
                        "The choice to take the card into the hand matters. Cascade could whiff, flipping a counterspell with nothing to counter, while discover always gives you something useful. And because discover is an action rather than a trigger on casting, it can be put on anything. {Trumpeting Carnosaur} discovers 5 when it enters. {Geological Appraiser} discovers 3 when it enters if you cast it, which is exactly the detail that got it banned in Pioneer, because copying it with cheap clone creatures set off the trigger again.",
                        "The common lands called caves carry discover too. {Hidden Volcano} and its four siblings enter tapped, tap for one colour, and later can be sacrificed to discover 4, so that late in a game a land turns back into a spell. The mechanic stands for the set's whole theme, the thrill of digging into the unknown and finding treasure.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-descend", "Descend",
                    listOf(
                        "Descending is a new piece of game vocabulary. Whenever a permanent card, meaning an artifact, battle, creature, enchantment, land or planeswalker card, is put into your graveyard from anywhere, you have descended. Instants and sorceries do not count. Cards can care whether you descended this turn, or trigger whenever you descend.",
                        "Two ability words build on it. Descend 4 and descend 8 switch an effect on only if there are at least that many permanent cards in your graveyard. Fathomless descent instead counts them, and grows as they pile up. {Squirming Emergence} uses fathomless descent to return a permanent from the graveyard to the battlefield, as long as its mana value is no greater than the count, and {The Mycotyrant} makes a Fungus at your end step for each time you descended that turn.",
                        "In Limited this drove two archetypes. Blue and black, Echoes of the Dead, was a descend control deck, and black and red, Going Down, was a descend beatdown deck. Black and green, Fungal Festering, was the grinding version. The mechanic is the set's sense of going ever deeper, told in cards, and it rewards decks that fill their own graveyards.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-craft", "Craft",
                    listOf(
                        "Craft is a new way for artifacts to transform. A card with craft names what it needs, such as craft with artifact, or craft with two creatures, and a mana cost. You pay the cost and exile the card itself along with the right materials, which you may take from your battlefield or your graveyard, and the card returns to the battlefield transformed. You can only do it at sorcery speed. The exiled materials can matter to the back face.",
                        "At common every colour has one, a cycle of artifacts that do something small when they enter and can later be crafted into something larger. {Oteclan Landmark} becomes a flying creature that lends its wings to other attackers, and {Tithing Blade} becomes a sepulchre that drains the opponent. At rare, a cycle of five legendary artifacts and enchantments turn into legendary lands, like {Thousand Moons Smithy}, which becomes the Barracks of the Thousand, and {Tarrian's Journal}, which becomes the Tomb of Aclazotz.",
                        "The original Ixalan sets had flip cards that turned into legendary lands too, and this set reprints one of them, {Growing Rites of Itlimoc}, as the green member of the cycle. White and blue in Limited, Oltec Ingenuity, was an artifact control deck built around crafting and the set's gnomes. Craft tells the story of the Oltec, who took the ruined war machines of the Fomori and made them into tools.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Though the Sun Empire and the River Heralds once came together to plant the sacred forest, its roots have been tended by the mycoids ever since.",
                        "Growing Rites of Itlimoc",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-explore-and-maps", "Explore and Maps",
                    listOf(
                        "Explore came back from the first Ixalan set. When a creature explores, you reveal the top card of your library. If it is a land you put it into your hand. Otherwise you put a +1/+1 counter on the creature and may put the revealed card into your graveyard, which in this set happily also counts toward descending. {Jadelight Spelunker} explores a number of times you choose when you cast it.",
                        "The set added Map tokens, a new predefined token. A Map is an artifact that says you may pay one, tap it and sacrifice it so that a target creature you control explores, but only at sorcery speed. Maps came from many places. {Cartographer's Companion} makes one, and the removal spell {Get Lost}, which destroys a creature, enchantment or planeswalker, hands its controller two Maps by way of apology.",
                        "Green and blue in Limited, River Herald Expedition, was the explore deck. The theme matches Amalia's blood maps and the Oltec habit of never getting lost in the Core. Explore and Maps together let a deck grow its creatures while sifting its library for what it needs.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-caves-and-gods", "Caves and Temples",
                    listOf(
                        "The set introduced a new land type, Cave. The five common hidden caves are the best known, but they are not the only lands to carry the type, and some other cards in the set reward you for having Caves. It is a small rule, but it gives the underground theme a home on the battlefield, and it means a deck can be built around the act of going down into the dark as well as the creatures it finds there.",
                        "The most striking design is the cycle of Deep Gods. Each of the five is a mythic legendary God creature that transforms into a land when it dies, returning as a temple, and each temple has an ability that turns it back into the god once its condition is met. Ojer Taq becomes the Temple of Civilization, Ojer Pakpatiq the Temple of Cyclical Time, Aclazotz the Temple of the Dead, Ojer Axonil the Temple of Power, and Ojer Kaslem the Temple of Cultivation. Killing one only buys time.",
                        "Beyond these, the set completes the Restless lands, the allied colour creature lands that began with Wilds of Eldraine, such as {Restless Anchorage}, which makes a Map when it attacks. There are also five common Dinosaurs that can be cycled for a land and typal payoffs for Dinosaurs, Pirates, Vampires and Merfolk, though the designers made a point that the set is not built around tribes the way the first visit was.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-archetypes", "The Archetypes",
                    listOf(
                        "Each colour pair in Limited had a theme with a story. White and blue was Oltec Ingenuity, artifact control. Blue and black was Echoes of the Dead, descend control. Black and red was Going Down, an aggressive descend deck. Red and green was Dinosaurs of the Sun Empire, big creatures stomping over everything. Green and white was Malamet Tactics, an aggressive deck full of buffs.",
                        "The other five pairs were the enemy colours. White and black was Dusk Legion Pilgrimage, built around sacrifice. Blue and red was Pirates' Plunder, an artifact aggro deck. Black and green was Fungal Festering, a grinding descend deck. Red and white was Thousand Moons Legion, a midrange deck that wanted to tap its creatures. Green and blue was River Herald Expedition, the explore deck.",
                        "Each pair had a gold uncommon signpost, among them {Kutzil, Malamet Exemplar}, {Caparocti Sunborn}, {Nicanzil, Current Conductor}, {Uchbenbak, the Great Mistake} and {Captain Storm, Cosmium Raider}, and a gold rare such as {Abuelo, Ancestral Echo}, {Palani's Hatcher}, {Molten Collapse} and {The Belligerent}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "To the mother lode, and death take the wary!",
                        "Captain Storm, Cosmium Raider",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Story", "Going Underground, Parts 1 and 2, by Mark Rosewater, Making Magic on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan Release Notes"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "lci-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-five-gods", "The Five Gods",
                    listOf(
                        "The five Deep Gods are the set's centrepiece and some of its best Commander cards. {Ojer Taq, Deepest Foundation} triples the creature tokens you would create, which made it an instant favourite in token decks. {Ojer Axonil, Deepest Might} raises small bits of red noncombat damage to its own power, so that every pinging spell hits like a god. {Ojer Pakpatiq, Deepest Epoch} gives your instants rebound, casting each one again next turn.",
                        "{Ojer Kaslem, Deepest Growth} digs into your library for creatures and lands when it deals combat damage. {Aclazotz, Deepest Betrayal} is a flying lifelinker that makes each opponent discard when it attacks and makes a Bat whenever they discard a land. All five come back as temples when they die, and each temple can become the god again. All five also have a showcase version in the Gods of Ixalan treatment.",
                        "The vampire god's card is the right one for the story. It is very hard to be rid of, and in the end the Oltec were not rid of him either.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-legends", "Legends of the Set",
                    listOf(
                        "Nearly everyone in the story has a card. {Huatli, Poet of Unity} finds a basic land and later flips into a saga about the Fifth People that makes Dinosaurs. {Quintorius Kand} is the planeswalker. {Saheeli, the Sun's Brilliance} copies artifacts and creatures. {Inti, Seneschal of the Sun}, {Caparocti Sunborn}, {Akal Pakal, First Among Equals} and {Anim Pakal, Thousandth Moon} fill out the Sun Empire and the Oltec. {Abuelo, Ancestral Echo} flickers creatures and artifacts, the way an Echo comes and goes.",
                        "On the darker side are {Vito, Fanatic of Aclazotz}, {Bartolomé del Presidio}, {Amalia Benavides Aguirre}, {The Mycotyrant} and {Xavier Sal, Infested Captain}, the poor mayor of Downtown. For the Malamet there are {Sovereign Okinec Ahau} and {Kutzil, Malamet Exemplar}, and for the Heralds {Nicanzil, Current Conductor}. The pirates have {Malcolm, Alluring Scoundrel}, {Breeches, Eager Pillager} and {Captain Storm, Cosmium Raider}.",
                        "Some legends come from the history rather than the story. {Tan Jolom, the Worldwalker} is the Komon who first walked around the Core, and {Chimil, the Inner Sun} is the sun herself, a card that stops your spells from being countered and discovers 5 at the end of each of your turns. {Gishath, Sun's Avatar} returned as a reprint, joined by {Itzquinth, Firstborn of Gishath}. The showcase Legends of Ixalan frame was used for many of these.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Quintorius touched the old poncho he'd found. A rush of magic condensed into an Echo, and wise Abuelo stepped forth.",
                        "Abuelo's Awakening",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-staples-and-bans", "Staples and Bans",
                    listOf(
                        "Several cards from the set became staples well beyond it. {Cavern of Souls} was reprinted, the land that makes creature spells of a chosen type uncounterable. {Get Lost} became one of the standard removal spells of its time. {Bonehoard Dracosaur} was widely played, a flying first striker that exiles two cards each upkeep for you to play and makes a Dinosaur or a Treasure along the way. {Trumpeting Carnosaur}, {Deep-Cavern Bat} and {Sorcerous Spyglass} all saw wide play.",
                        "Three cards were later banned. {Geological Appraiser} was banned in Pioneer and Explorer in December 2023, because its enter trigger could be copied by cheap clones to combo on the third turn. {Amalia Benavides Aguirre} was banned in Pioneer on August 26, 2024, after she and Wildgrowth Walker became a combo deck that could swing a twenty power creature into an empty board or, if Walker became indestructible, draw the game at will.",
                        "{Abuelo's Awakening} was banned in Standard in June 2025 when Foundations brought Omniscience into the format, because blue decks could mill Omniscience and return it on curve far too reliably. And on Arena at release, a bug in Fabrication Foundry let it return more than it should, so it was briefly banned there until the bug was fixed.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-treatments", "Treatments and Guests",
                    listOf(
                        "The set looked spectacular in its premium versions. There were showcase frames for the legends and for the gods, a borderless Classic Dinosaurs treatment, a borderless Oltec style for spells, lands and Quint himself, borderless versions of the Restless lands, and full art basic lands. The neon ink treatment from Kamigawa returned on {Cavern of Souls}, printed in six different colours of neon.",
                        "The set also introduced Special Guests, a new sheet of eighteen highly desirable reprints with art and flavor matched to the plane, found in Set and Collector boosters. Among them was {Mana Crypt}, which also came in neon ink. There was also a series of twenty borderless box topper promos called the Treasure Trove, all but one of them reprints from outside the Ixalan sets.",
                        "On top of all that, Set and Collector boosters and bundles carried the Jurassic World Collection, a Universes Beyond insert of original card designs from the Jurassic World films, much as the Transformers had been inserted into The Brothers' War. The buy-a-box promo was {Jadelight Spelunker}, and the bundle promo was {Hit the Mother Lode}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lci-the-commander-decks", "The Commander Decks",
                    listOf(
                        "Four Commander decks came out with the set. Ahoy Mateys is a blue, black and red pirate deck led by {Admiral Brass, Unsinkable}, the Coalition's governor, who drags dead pirates back into battle. Blood Rites is a white and black vampire deck led by {Clavileño, First of the Blessed}, who turns attacking vampires into demons. Explorers of the Deep is a green and blue merfolk deck led by {Hakbal of the Surging Soul}, all about exploring. Veloci-Ramp-Tor is a red, green and white dinosaur deck led by {Pantlaza, Sun-Favored}, Huatli's raptor, who discovers whenever a dinosaur enters.",
                        "The Commander set added new legends too. {Wayta, Trainer Prodigy} makes creatures fight and doubles the triggers that fire when your creatures are hurt. {Timothar, Baron of Bats} turns dying vampires into bats that bring them back. {Xolatoyac, the Smiling Flood} floods lands into Islands. Many old legends from the first Ixalan sets were reprinted beside them, Pirate captains, vampire saints and elder dinosaurs among them.",
                        "The decks leaned on the typal themes far more heavily than the main set did, giving each of the four great factions of the surface world a deck of its own. Admiral Brass's pirates, Clavileño's vampires, Hakbal's merfolk and Pantlaza's dinosaurs line up exactly with the Brazen Coalition, the Legion of Dusk, the River Heralds and the Sun Empire. The raptor who knocked Huatli over like an eager puppy in the first scene of the story ended up leading a Commander deck of his own.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "A First Look at The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Banned and Restricted Announcement, June 30, 2025"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
